<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cécile Fatiman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/1791">1791</a>–<a href="/years/1845">1845</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Cécil Attiman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cécile Fatiman was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou</a> priestess (manbo) and revolutionary who, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutty-boukman">Dutty Boukman</a>, led the ceremony at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bois-caiman">Bois Caïman</a> that marked the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>. The daughter of an enslaved African woman and a Corsican prince, she and her mother were bought and sold as slaves, and she gained her freedom before or during the rising of 1791. On the night of 21–22 August 1791 she presided over the ceremony, which gathered 200 enslaved people: a black pig was sacrificed, and those present swore to kill the white slavers and to obey Boukman as their commander. She later married the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-louis-pierrot">Jean-Louis Pierrot</a> and reportedly died at the age of 112.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Her origins are uncertain. The Haitian historian Rodney Salnave believed her father to have been a son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-von-neuhoff">Theodore of Corsica</a> and her birth name Cécil Attiman. Aisha Khan held that she was a Muslim and that Fatiman was cognate with the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aime-cesaire">Aimé Césaire</a> thought her Kongolese, a view David Patrick Geggus questions because it leaves the name unexplained. She is also believed to have been related to Marie-Louise Coidavid, later Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>. Described as a mulatto with green eyes, she was sold with her mother from childhood, and her two brothers disappeared after being sold apart from them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to the historian Aisha K. Finch, Fatiman reshaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> ideals of liberty, equality and fraternity for the Haitian setting, upholding black women's bodily integrity and property rights, and saw spirit possession as the opposite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, a moment in which the possessed became gods. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bois-caiman">Bois Caïman</a>, the Alligator Wood, the gathering took place in a thunderstorm in the forests of the north, and she was said to have been possessed by one of the Èzili, believed to be Dantò. Garvey F. Lundy understood the sacrifice as a Petwo rite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou">Vodou</a>. Those present drank the pig's blood before swearing their oath, and during the revolution she and other manbos were credited with giving the fighters superhuman courage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Henri Christophe</a> established the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, she married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-louis-pierrot">Jean-Louis Pierrot</a>, a general and a prince under the monarchy, and they had a daughter, Marie Louise Amélia Célestine. The couple later divorced; Pierrot became President of Haiti in 1845, for 10 months. She lived out her life in Le Cap, in good health to the end.</p><p>She entered the record through Antoine Dalmas, a plantation doctor who observed the ceremony, and little archival evidence of her life survives. Étienne Charlier found her in the oral history of the revolution, and family records kept by her grandson Pierre Benoit Rameau confirmed her part. Historians relying on colonial documents, such as Jean Fouchard, left her out, and Léon-François Hoffman and Franck Sylvain contested that the ceremony took place, but Carolyn Fick held it to be historical and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou">Vodou</a> in character. C. L. R. James rewrote her as the priestess Celestine in his play of 1934.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cécile Fatiman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The priestess <b>Cécile Fatiman</b> presided with Boukman over the ceremony at Bois Caïman in August 1791; Micaela Bastidas commanded rebels beside her husband Túpac Amaru II and was executed in Cusco in 1781; and in 1900 Yaa Asantewaa led the last armed resistance to British rule in Asante.</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li><li><blockquote>In August 1791 the priestess <b>Cécile Fatiman</b> presided with Boukman over the ceremony at Bois Caïman.</blockquote><a href="/societies/saint-domingue">Social Hierarchy of Saint-Domingue</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cap-haitien">Cap-Haïtien</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/haiti">Haiti</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.75,-72.2&amp;year=1818" aria-label="Cap-Haïtien on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2601 1432 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Cécile Fatiman"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3051 1702h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3051 1702h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=19.75,-72.2&amp;year=1818">What was here in 1818? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
